 spike in the  number of impacts  around four billion years ago okay  so what could cause a sudden spike in  the number of impactors  traveling to the inner solar system and  hitting our moon in other locations  we would normally expect a steady  decline in the number of impactors over  time  if there was a big increase in the  number of impacts  then perhaps there was also a big but  more  temporary disturbance something big that  perturbed a much larger number of  impactors inwards from the asteroid belt  and kuiper belt  if we want a big disturbance the biggest  planets especially jupiter are the  natural place to look  computer simulations allow us to study  how the giant planets formed  and gravitationally interacted early in  the solar system  these simulations demonstrate that the  giant planets can migrate their orbits  somewhat  during their formation this idea that  giant planets like jupiter and satellite  actually migrate was a huge surprise  but the idea makes more sense after  studying thousands of exoplanets  orbiting other stars  sometimes those gas giant planets  migrate  very close to their parent stars in our  own solar system  we don't really know what the original  configuration of the giant's planets was  just after they formed but we can run  lots of simulations with different  possibilities  and see which ones eventually evolve  into the system we see today  one model that does a very nice job is  the so-called nice model  nice here refers to the city of nice  france because the model was initially  developed by  scientists at an astronomical  observatory near nice  so the nice model begins with the four  giant planets  jupiter saturn uranus and neptune  situated more closely in space  than they are today their orbits were  more compact  fitting all four planets between about 5  and 17  times earth's distance from the sun  compare that to today  where jupiter resides at five but saturn  is at nine  uranus at 19 and neptune is all the way  out at  30 times earth's distance from the sun  during this early time a disk of icy  planetesimals where the total mass of  tens of earth masses would have existed  out beyond where the giant planets were  forming  out there the orbital speeds were too  slow and the material too sparse to form  a giant planet  every once in a while an icy  planetesimal from this disk  would be perturbed into the region of  the giant planets  but when one of these planetesimals is  scattered inwards by a planet  the giant planet's orbit is pushed a bit  outwards momentum must be conserved  it's similar to why pushing yourself off  a skateboard in one direction  means the skateboard goes in the other  direction  so now picture one of these  planetesimals first encountering neptune  on its way in  pushing neptune outward a tiny bit as  the planetesimal moves inwards  then the planetesimal gets to uranus and  scattered inwards  even more moving uranus out a tiny bit  and the same thing happens yet again  when the planetesimal encounters saturn  now admittedly these outward  perturbations of the giant planets would  be extremely small  and not every encounter would result in  an inward scattering of the planetesimal  other directions are still possible but  on average  the odds are better that the  planetesimal gets scattered inwards  when encountering neptune uranus and  saturn  and if successive planetesimals are  getting scattered inward over and over  again  then the combined effect can be to move  the planet's orbits outwards  but something different happens when the  inward scattered planetesimal finally  reaches jupiter  jupiter is so massive that jupiter is  more likely  on average to fling the planetesimal  into a highly elliptical orbit  taking the object further outward in the  solar system but every time a  planetesimal is flung  outwards then jupiter migrates inwards a  bit  so jupiter moves inwards while saturn  uranus and neptune each move outwards  and all this continues to go on for some  time  until jupiter and saturn end up in a  very special configuration  as jupiter is moving inwards its orbital  period is decreasing  as saturn's orbit is moving outwards its  orbital period is increasing  eventually they reach a state where  jupiter completes exactly  two whole orbits every time saturn  completes one  they are now in a two to one orbit orbit  resonance  what this resonance means is that  jupiter and saturn can gravitationally  influence each other more  and in a quite regular way and what  happens  is they also perturb each other into  orbits that are less circular and more  elliptical  that means that jupiter and saturn cross  through a larger range of orbital  distances  during their orbits but taking the two  biggest planets in the solar system  and giving them orbits that are less  circular and causing them to visit  new places can cause all hell to break  loose among all the remaining  planetesimals in the solar system  saturn quickly moves to a new position  closer to where we find it today  out around 10 astronomical units 10  times earth's distance from the sun  but if saturn moves then this has  implications for uranus and neptune  gravitational interactions with saturn  move uranus and neptune  onto more elliptical orbits of their own  pushing them  to cross farther out in the solar system  but as uranus and neptune migrated to  orbits farther from the sun  that destabilized the disk of small  bodies out near pluto  some of those icy planetesimals from  what is called the kuiper belt  would be thrown into the inner solar  system producing a spike in bombardment  of the inner planets and about half the  time when you run simulations with the  nice model  neptune and uranus switch places during  their outward migration  in these versions of the model neptune  was originally closer to the sun than  uranus  the reverse of what we have today this  scenario is appealing because neptune is  almost 18  more massive than uranus for a given  composition  we would have expected all other things  being equal for neptune  the more massive to form closer to the  sun where the protoplanetary disk was  denser  anyway the end scenario is the giant  planets  where we see them today preceded by  movement of the giant planets  that caused increased bombardment in the  early solar system  a lot depends on small details in the  initial conditions of the models  but many possibilities lead to a major  destabilization migration  of the giant planets and flinging of  planetesimals  into the inner solar system and there's  even more  but this gives you an idea i love how  the nice model  opens our eyes to possibilities the  orbits of the giant planets  dance around the solar system as the  giants migrate  and the model is able to explain some  previously taken for granted features we  see in the solar system  but we don't know for sure that the  model describes what actually happened  even the late heavy bombardment one of  the major consequences that made the  nice model so attractive  may not be real that's because  there is some debate over evidence from  the moon rocks supporting the claim that  there was a spike in large impacts  around 4 billion years ago  the debate stems from looking at the  locations where we got our lunar samples  during the apollo missions after all it  was these lunar samples  that provided the first and strongest  evidence that a late heavy series of  large impacts occurred  in a relatively short geologic time  but it's possible that all of these moon  rock samples are  actually sampling from just one very  large impact crater on the moon  imbrium that's not because we collected  all the samples from a single location  but because the imbrium impact would  have spread ejecta  over large distances surrounding the  basin and since imbrium is the youngest  of the large impact basins  the ejecta would have landed on top  could it be possible  that samples we collected from six  different locations on the moon  all happened to be ejected from imbrium  if so  that would explain but a very different  way  while the ages in the samples cluster  around the age of imbrium  all those impacts still happened it's  just that they may not have happened so  close together in time  instead it may be that there was a more  steady and less dramatic flux of  impactors from the beginning of the  solar system  4.5 billion years ago down to about 3.5  billion years ago  without a spike centered at 4 billion  years ago  and the fact that we don't see a lot of  impact milk crystals older than about  4.2 billion years  maybe simply because older material was  more likely to be pulverized by later  impacts  perhaps virtually all the older evidence  has been destroyed  so which is it was there a concentrated  bombardment caused by a dance of the  giant planets  or not perhaps the most important thing  that will help are more samples from the  moon  from a wider variety of locations and  different impact basins  after all the ages of surface features  on mars  mercury and venus are all anchored to  ages of craters determined from lunar  samples  combined with counting and comparing the  relative number of craters on each world  if more samples from the moon resulted  in a correction to the ages previously  determined for lunar features  that would cause to revise the timing  and sequence of events throughout the  inner solar system  the timing of events early in the solar  system may be  fundamentally changed by better  knowledge of lunar impact basins  bringing samples back from the moon now  seems so easy  we did it during the apollo program and  yet no samples have been brought back to  earth from the moon  since the mid-1970s naturally  scientists have lots of hopes and plans  for new samples from the moon  the more samples the better a picture we  can develop about the timing of lunar  impacts and the solar system as a whole  we need to return to the moon to sample  from other locations on the moon  that have no chance of containing the  ejecta from the relatively young  imbrium basin we need a bigger  collection of moon rocks to tell us when  and whether a concentrated period of  late heavy bombardment  even happened at all and once we confirm  or revise when and whether a big  increase in impacts occurred  that will confirm whether the giant  planets really did go  dancing around the solar system  disrupting all the smaller bodies and  causing a time  with a huge increase in impact craters  in short  more moon rocks are going to tell us how  the solar system formed  [Music]  you

